  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & ={e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\cdot {e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+2\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n2.1{e}^{0.2}=f(2.1,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(2.1,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $(2,0)$ to $(2.1,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2.1-2=0.1, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\cdot (0.1)+2\\cdot (0.2) \\\\  & ≈0.1+0.4 \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.5.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.1,0.2) & ≈f(2,0)+0.5 \\\\ 2.1{e}^{0.2} & ≈2{e}^{0}+0.5 \\\\  & ≈2+0.5 \\\\  & ≈2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
